                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Short version: 61%[/h1]\nThe cute exterior of Button Tales hides a devilishly difficult match-3 game with a strange mechanic: you can not only swap but also flip the tiles, which creates complex, although sometimes chaotic scenarios.\n\n[h1]Long version:[/h1]\nSome match-3 games rely on luck, some rely on speed, but I cannot recall any other match-3 game like Button Tales, which relies on the player’s memory. Its main feature that sets it apart from its uncountable siblings is that you cannot just swap neighbouring tiles to create matches, but you can also flip them between two pre-determined states.\nThis feature is an essential part of gameplay, because all 100+ levels limit the amount of swaps the player can make, meaning we simply must rely on tile flipping to progress.\n\nCombine this restriction with the usual match-3 obstacles and some extremely tricky level layouts, and we suddenly find an almost frustratingly challenging game. The child-friendly graphics and design may suggest otherwise, but finishing Button Tales is a long, difficult journey, something that should occupy match-3 enthusiast for quite a while.",
